From b81ddb074e31f605b342c37a445a1553498cd118 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 07:04:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] search a folder where it is, however its path is spelled

A search of one folder looked the folder up in the index under the
names it was given by. An index has a folder under the names the disk
gives it on the way down from its search location, so a folder given
any other way was searched and had nothing in it. That is a folder
with `..` in its path, as `fffinder ../lib --query parser --here`
gives it on macOS and Linux, and Go to Folder with `..` on every
system. It is a folder given or opened through a symbolic link inside
a search location, which the index doesn't follow unless Follow
symbolic links is on. And on a disk that takes a name in either
letter case, as most on macOS and Windows do, it is a folder typed in
another case than the disk's, such as `~/documents`. The window
listed the folder and Search Only in "lib" was on, and every search
said No Results, by name and by contents. `fff find --here` and
`fff search --here` found nothing in a folder the shell was in
through a link, and `scope` in `/v1/find` and `/v1/search` nothing
for any of the three. Only a folder that didn't look as if it were in
a search location had its path resolved, to see whether it led into
one.

The engine now asks the disk where the folder is before each search
of one folder, and searches it by the names the index has. A folder
that is really in a search location is spelled the way the location
is, and from there on by its real names. One that only looks as if
it were in one, because a link or a `..` leads out of it, is searched
by its real path through a temporary index, like any other folder
outside the search locations. A folder spelled the way the index has
it is searched as before, and so is one that neither looks as if it
were in a search location nor is in one. An index that follows links
has a linked folder under the link's name as well, so with Follow
symbolic links on, such a folder is still searched by that name; a
`..`, or a path that is the folder's own but for its letter case, is
resolved there too.

A search respells its folder once and goes on. It used to call itself
with the new spelling, which ended because that spelling began with a
search location. Whether a second call would respell again now
depends on what the disk answers, so there is none. `Engine::prepare`
waits for the index that the search will use.

Only Linux was run, where a name in another letter case is another
folder. That half is tested as far as Linux allows: the rule by
itself, told where the disk says each folder is, and a search of
`docs` for a folder `Docs` that runs only where the disk has the two
as one, which no disk here does.

Changelog (Fixed): **Search Only in "Folder name"** found nothing in
a folder that the window had reached by anything but its plain path:
with `..` in the path, as after `fffinder ../lib` on macOS and Linux
or Go to Folder with `..`; through a symbolic link inside a search
location, while **Follow symbolic links** is off, as it is at first;
or, on macOS and Windows, by a path typed in another letter case than
the folder's, such as `~/documents`. The window showed the folder's
items, and every search of it said No Results. Such a folder is now
searched where it really is, and the results show that place. A
linked folder that leads outside the search locations gets a
temporary index, like any folder there. `--here`, `fff find` and
`fff search` with `--scope` or `--here`, and `scope` in `/v1/find`
and `/v1/search` follow the same rule.

